  "notes": "Root cause of bench_ooo_overlap64: the single-entry LSU held lsu_disp_ready=0 for the whole external-memory wait, so any second memory instruction sitting in ID blocked rename id_ready / the whole front end; no younger independent ALU could ever be dispatched or issued while the older load waited (patching rename/IQ/ROB could not help). Fix: load_store_unit is now a 4-entry in-order memory queue. Only the head issues to the dcache with address/data held stable until req_ready (req_active latch, still one outstanding request), the response is consumed exactly once and epoch-qualified (stale responses from squashed requests are ignored), stores still issue only after store_commit, and memory operations still issue strictly in program order. Flush squashes the younger suffix using the ROB range test and drops any pending request. rv32i5's lsu_mem_pending now also reflects queued LSU occupancy (!lsu_idle), so dbg_ooo_issue/dbg_ooo_bypass are derived from real IQ issue/completion versus ROB head while a memory op is pending (no counters/constants). Timing corners verified: reset values for all new state, one retire pulse per committed instruction (ROB/commit untouched), halted/exit_code commit cycle unchanged (ECALL only commits after all older memory ops drained), stall/flush valid handling (dispatch gated by !halted/!flush, squashed entries never issue), and one-cycle response capture with ready/valid stability on the external dmem interface."
}
